hand has no honors.  \n\nMaybe switch strategy: try to form pung of T3 (2 left available) and pair with W5 or T5/T6, then hope for patterns like All Pungs? But I have three chows exposed, so not All Pungs.  \n\nActually, All Pungs requires four pungs/kongs; I have three chows exposed, so cannot get All Pungs.  \n\nSo my patterns limited due to exposed chows.  \n\nGiven this, I might be playing defensively, avoid giving points to others.  \n\nEvaluation:  \nValid actions: discard one tile from hand.  \n\nOptions:  \n1. Play W5: Discard W5, keep T3,T3,T5,T6. Leaves me with two T3 (potential pung) and T5,T6 (potential chow with T4 or T7). But T4 gone, so only T7 left (unshown T7:0). So T5,T6 dead for chow. Better keep W5?  \n2. Play T5: Discard T5, keep W5, T3,T3,T6. Then possible: W5 chow with W3,W4 or W6,W7; or T3 pung; or T6 chow with T4,T5 or T7,T8 (but T4 gone, T5 discarded, T7 0 left, T8 1 left, so T6-T7-T8 impossible, T5-T6-T7 impossible). So T6 not useful.  \n3. Play T3: Discard one T3, keep W5, T3, T5, T6. Then only one T3 (so no pung possible). Could aim for chow W3-W4-W5 or W5-W6-W7; or chow T4-T5-T6 (T4 gone) or T5-T6-T7 (T7 0). So not good.  \n4. Play T6: Discard T6, keep W5, T3,T3,T5. Then T3 pung possible; T5 could pair with something; W5 for chow.  \n\nGiven I need a meld + pair, best is to keep tiles that can form meld quickly. T3 pung is easiest (need one T3). Unshown T3: 2 left, so chance okay. If I keep T3,T3, then I need one more T3 to complete pung meld, then remaining two tiles (W5 and T5) must form a pair (need another W5 or T5). Unshown W5:2 left, T5:2 left, so possible.  \n\nIf I discard T6, then I have T3,T3 (potential pung), W5 and T5 (potential pair with draw of W5 or T5). That\u2019s better.  \n\nIf I discard W5, then I have T3,T3 (pung), T5,T6 (dead unless draw T4 or T7, both scarce). Worse.  \n\nIf I discard T5, I have T3,T3 (pung), W5,T6 (dead for meld, could pair W5 or T6).  \n\nIf I discard one T3, I lose pung chance.  \n\nSo best to keep T3,T3. Between W5 and T5/T6: T5 is bamboo, same suit as T3, but no sequence possible with T3. W5 is different suit, but could pair.  \n\nActually, for pair flexibility: W5 and T5 both have 2 unshown each, equal chance.  \n\nBut if I keep T5 and T6, they might form chow with drawn T4 or T7, but T4 gone, T7 0, so impossible. So T6 is useless. So discard T6.  \n\nReason:  \nDiscarding T6 preserves T3 pair (potential pung) and keeps W5 and T5 as potential pair tiles. It removes a tile that is less useful (T6) because its possible chow partners (T4, T5, T7, T8) are either gone or scarce (T7 0 left, T4 0 left, T5 2 left but T5 is in hand, but T5-T6 need T4 or T7 for chow, not possible). So T6 is isolated.  \n\nThus, discard T6.  \n\nAnswer: Play T6"}
